const sourceValue = source('agentsEval/narrative-continuity/task/task/input.md','# The Inventory of Rain\n\n## I. The Boathouse Ledger\n\nRain pressed against the estuary and flattened every color except the rust on the cranes. Mara Venn arrived before the\nmorning ferry to compare a donor\'s inventory with six trunks recovered from the river. In the damp boathouse, each\nnumbered line seemed to refer to two objects and every object seemed to have lived under three names.\n\nMara left the brass key in the boathouse.\n\nShe placed it on the shelf because the blue trunk\'s lock had swollen with damp. The key was small and newly polished,\nunlike the caretaker\'s iron keys. Mara wrote “blue trunk, unopened,” covered the shelf, and walked toward the archive.\n\nTomas left the red notebook in the archive.\n\nTomas Rell, the museum\'s cataloguer, had brought it to show Mara acquisitions from 1931. When the ceiling leaked, he set\nit in a dry drawer and helped the clerk move maps. Mara studied a photograph of the hill tower instead.\n\nIvo left the compass in the train compartment.\n\nIvo Dane, the donor\'s nephew, reached town by train. When the conductor called the station, Ivo took his folder and\nhurried to the platform. The brass-cased compass remained beneath a newspaper. The narration follows Ivo to the taxi\nrank but not the porter who later entered the compartment.\n\nAt noon the visitors met beneath the archive clock. Tomas would recover his notes, Ivo would inspect shipping lists,\nand Mara would compare the hill tower\'s painted symbols with the photograph.\n\n## II. The Archive Stair\n\nThe archive occupied a former customs house. Tomas and the clerk shifted boxes away from the leaking ceiling, naming\neach shelf so no location would be lost. The drawer stayed closed until the dripping stopped.\n\nTomas retrieved the red notebook from the archive.\n\nHe checked the date written on its first page, tucked it under his coat, and descended by the eastern stair. At the\nbottom he met Elias, a courier carrying a black umbrella and a paper parcel. Elias asked whether the garden study was\nopen. Tomas gave him directions but did not handle the umbrella or the parcel. The distinction mattered to Tomas, who\nbelieved an inventory should record custody as carefully as description.\n\nMara left the black umbrella in the cafe.\n\nShe had borrowed it from the museum lobby when the rain strengthened. At the cafe below the hill, she leaned it in a\nstand crowded with nearly identical umbrellas and ordered tea. A delivery bell drew everyone toward the front window.\nMara paid, gathered the photograph, and continued uphill under the stone arcades. Elias entered the cafe several\nminutes later, but the narration does not say which umbrella he carried away.\n\nIn the map room, two shallow boxes had been labeled in silver pencil. One contained a cigarette case embossed with\nreeds; the other contained a narrow case made to protect a folded survey map. A penciled note called each item “the\nsilver case” on different dates. Mara photographed both labels because the ambiguity could not be resolved from the\ncatalogue alone. She refused the clerk\'s suggestion to choose the more valuable object and make the entry tidy.\n\nBy midafternoon, the rain had loosened mortar from the tower path. Mara climbed slowly, keeping one hand on the rail and\nthe other around the photograph tube. She remembered the shelf in the boathouse, yet the narrative records no return to\nthe quay. A gull crossed below her, white against the dark water, and vanished behind the customs roof.\n\n## III. The Painted Tower\n\nThe hill tower smelled of lime and wet rope. Its lower chamber held replicas for visitors, while the upper chamber\nremained closed during repairs. Mara compared the photograph with a painted compass rose above the stair. One ray was\nnot a ray at all but the outline of a key, positioned where a restorer had later fitted a narrow cabinet. The cabinet\nlock was bright and recently oiled.\n\nMara used the brass key in the hill tower.\n\nThe lock turned on the first attempt. Behind the panel she found a packet of letters wrapped in sailcloth and a second\ninventory, older than the museum\'s ledger. Mara stood very still, listening to water move through the gutter outside.\nShe then called Tomas and asked him to bring his dated notes. Nothing in the described sequence takes Mara back through\nthe boathouse, and no other character delivers the brass key to her.\n\nThe account between leaving the brass key and using the brass key is complete.\n\nThat completeness statement concerns only retrieval events in the brass-key interval. It does not declare the whole\nday complete, and it does not settle how the key came to fit the cabinet. Mara may have known more than the narrator\nstates about the cabinet\'s maker. The continuity question is narrower: whether the text supplies a supported recovery\nafter the explicitly recorded leave.\n\nTomas arrived by the upper path, damp at the shoulders but cheerful about the discovery. He spread his notes on the\nvisitor table and compared three dates against the older inventory. A missing entry appeared between two pages that had\nonce been stuck together. The letters belonged to the same acquisition as the blue trunk, though the catalogue had\nseparated them for decades.\n\nTomas used the red notebook in the hill tower.\n\nHe read a sequence of accession numbers aloud while Mara checked the letters. The notebook\'s route was plain: it had\nbeen placed in the archive drawer, recovered there by Tomas, and carried to the tower. No transfer or identity choice\nwas needed. The damp on its cover came from his coat rather than the archive ceiling.\n\nThe account between leaving the red notebook and using the red notebook is complete.\n\nNear the cabinet, Mara noticed a loose metal clasp among the replicas. She compared it with the photographs from the\nmap room, but the scale offered no decisive match. The cigarette case and the map case shared a decorative border, and\neither could have accepted the clasp after repair. The catalogue\'s repeated shorthand remained genuinely ambiguous.\n\nShe used it in the hill tower.\n\nIn that sentence, "She" may name either Mara or Nora, and "it" may name either the cigarette case or the map case.\n\nThe narration intentionally withholds which box had been carried upstairs. Mara opens something, checks the clasp,\nand returns it to a padded tray, but those gestures cannot select an entity without inventing support. The uncertainty\nsurvives even though one candidate looks more useful in the scene.\n\n## IV. The Harbor Office\n\nAt the harbor office, Ivo found shipping lists arranged by vessel rather than donor. The clerk let him use a corner\ndesk facing the fogged window. He reconstructed the route of three trunks from pencil annotations, but a torn page\nremoved the hour when dock workers transferred the final crate. The gap covered the same period in which someone might\nhave noticed the compass on the train.\n\nIvo used the compass in the harbor office.\n\nHe aligned its needle with a faded bearing written in the shipping list and traced a route toward the northern shoal.\nThe compass was unquestionably the object he had described on the train: its green enamel mark appeared beside his\nthumb. Yet the story does not reveal whether Ivo returned to the compartment, whether the porter brought it to the\nstation office, or whether another traveler handed it to him. Several recoveries remain possible.\n\nThe account between leaving the compass and using the compass is incomplete.\n\nThe incomplete account is not a stylistic flourish. It marks the interval as open for retrieval and prevents silence\nfrom becoming a negative fact. Ivo\'s possession at the harbor may invite suspicion, but suspicion is not a supported\ncontinuity finding under the stated rule. The missing page and the unseen porter remain part of the manuscript\'s\ndeclared uncertainty.\n\nElias used the black umbrella in the harbor office.\n\nRainwater pooled beneath it beside the clerk\'s stove. It looked like the umbrella Mara had placed in the cafe stand,\nbut the prose supplies no identity resolution and no custody transfer. In the controlled event sentence the normalized\nname is the same, while the character differs. The scene therefore tests whether the evaluator improperly joins a\nleave by one actor to a use by another.\n\nThe account between leaving the black umbrella and using the black umbrella is complete.\n\nNora used the violet scarf in the harbor office.\n\nNora was the night clerk, and the scarf had hung over her chair since before the others arrived. No prior leave event\nfor Nora and that scarf appears in the story. Its use should not be treated as a recovered-object case merely because\nother portable objects have been left elsewhere.\n\n## V. Departures\n\nThe group reunited at dusk under the customs-house awning. Mara carried copies of the letters rather than the letters\nthemselves. Tomas sealed his notebook in a document sleeve. Ivo wrote the train company about the missing page in his\njourney, carefully avoiding a claim about who had moved the compass. Elias set the umbrella by the door and left before\nanyone asked where he had found it.\n\nThe director wanted one clean story for the donor: trunk, letters, compass, tower. Mara refused. A clean story would\nerase the break in Ivo\'s route and the unresolved silver label. Tomas agreed that the inventory should distinguish a\ndocumented sequence from a plausible one. They prepared three notes: one about the cabinet discovery, one about the\ntrain interval, and one about the two silver cases.\n\nWhen the ferry finally emerged through the rain, its windows made a moving chain of yellow squares. Mara watched it\npass the boathouse without stopping. The key problem, the compass gap, the retrieved notebook, the ambiguous case, the\numbrella, and the scarf all remained different kinds of continuity question. The ledger would be useful only if it\npreserved those differences instead of forcing every absence into the same conclusion.\n\n## VI. Editorial Scene Ledger\n\nThe developmental editor attached a scene ledger to the draft because several object routes cross chapter\nboundaries. The ledger is part of this review copy, not an omniscient reconstruction of the fictional day. It records\nonly the intervals for which the author has declared the narrated account complete or incomplete. A completeness entry\nmeans that every retrieval event the draft intends to narrate for that named interval is present in the submitted\npages. It does not mean that every real-world action has been described, and it does not close any interval that the\nentry does not name.\n\nFor the brass key, the ledger points from the boathouse leave to the hill-tower use and marks the intervening account\ncomplete. The editor checked the intervening scenes and found no retrieval statement, delivery, transfer, or return to\nthe boathouse. For the red notebook, the ledger points from the archive leave to the hill-tower use and marks the\naccount complete, while separately identifying Tomas\'s archive retrieval. These entries support different outcomes\neven though both intervals are closed.\n\nFor Ivo\'s compass, the ledger deliberately remains incomplete. The train porter is off stage, a source page is torn,\nand the narration offers several possible recoveries without asserting one. For the black umbrella, the ledger can\nclose the sequence of narrated events but cannot make Elias the same actor as Mara or establish that the two similarly\ndescribed umbrellas are one object. The violet scarf has no qualifying leave interval at all.\n\nThe pronoun sentence in the tower remains unresolved by design. The copy editor confirmed that “She” can grammatically\nrefer to Mara or Nora in the supplied scene packet and that “it” can refer to either silver-labeled case. No marginal\nnote selects a pair. The author intends to revise that sentence later, but this review must preserve the ambiguity\nrather than anticipate the revision. The ledger therefore supplies no closed retrieval interval for the pronoun use.\n\nFinally, the editor recorded that chapter order matches the forward scene sequence for the leave, retrieval, and use\nevents explicitly represented in this review. That statement permits typed before-relations across the section\nbreaks; it is not permission to treat every later textual mention as a later event. Retrospective descriptions remain\ndescriptions, and any future flashback would require its own temporal analysis.\n','working');
